blurb:
Challenge: Target Windows x86-64. rcx = long long *arr, edx = int count (Microsoft x64 calling convention). Reverse this function to equivalent C.

func:
    xor   eax, eax        ; sum = 0
    test  edx, edx
    jle   done
loop_top:
    mov   r8, [rcx]
    test  r8, r8
    js    skip
    add   rax, r8
skip:
    add   rcx, 8
    dec   edx
    jnz   loop_top
done:
    ret

What does this function compute, and what does it return in rax?

Answer:

long long sum_positive(long long *arr, int count) {
    long long sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        if (arr[i] >= 0) {
            sum += arr[i];
        }
    }
    return sum;
}

Explanation: rax accumulates the sum, seeded to zero, with an early jle bailout when count <= 0. Each iteration loads one 8-byte element through rcx (advanced by add rcx, 8, i.e. arr[i]), and test r8, r8 / js skip uses the sign flag to skip negative elements without a real comparison. dec edx / jnz counts count down to zero, so the loop runs exactly count times regardless of how many elements were skipped.
